      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare polysomnography (PSG) and self-reported sleep, symptoms (pain and fatigue), and anxiety between children with active and inactive juvenile rheumatoid arthritis (JRA) and examine relations among sleep, symptoms, and anxiety."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Two consecutive nights of PSG, self-reported sleep, and symptoms were obtained in 70 children 6-11 years of age with active (n = 35) or inactive (n = 35) JRA."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "On the second (study) night, PSG and self-reported sleep variables were not different, but pain and fatigue were significantly higher (both p <.02) in children with active compared to inactive disease. In a stepwise regression, age, medications, disease status, anxiety, evening pain, total sleep time, and arousals explained 36% of the variance in fatigue and age, disease status, and evening pain were significant (all p <.04) predictors of fatigue. All children showed longer sleep latency and reduced sleep efficiency on the first night in the laboratory."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Sleep was not altered in children with active JRA, however, the \"first night effect\" suggests that valid laboratory sleep assessments require an adaptation night."
        }
      ]},
